      {
        "name": "RunningCommand",
        "kind": "class",
        "module": "cuprum._process_handle.state",
        "path": "cuprum/_process_handle/state.py",
        "line": 58,
        "display": "class RunningCommand(run: _HandleRun) -> None",
        "doc": {
          "summary": "An owned handle to a command that is already running.",
          "body": [
            "Returned by <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/commands/#SafeCmd\"><code>SafeCmd.start</code></a>. Unlike <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/commands/#SafeCmd\"><code>SafeCmd.run</code></a>, which awaits a child to completion, this handle keeps the child alive and hands the caller explicit control over it: <code>wait</code> for the outcome, <code>signal</code> to deliver a POSIX signal, <code>terminate</code> for the graceful stop, and <code>kill</code> for the immediate one.",
            "The handle owns the child's lifetime. Leaving an <code>async with</code> block settles it deterministically — a child still running is stopped down the same <code>SIGTERM</code>, cancel-grace, <code>SIGKILL</code> path a cancelled <code>run()</code> takes — so no child is left running or unreaped because a caller forgot to stop it.",
            "Every operation that settles is idempotent and safe to repeat, and none of them suppresses the exception of an enclosing block. Repeating is safe on both halves of the operation: the outcome the settlement records is shared, and the stop that produces it is serialized, so two callers stopping the same child escalate it once between them however they interleave. Once the child has exited, <code>result</code> holds the same <code>CommandResult</code> a <code>run()</code> call would have returned, and further waits or terminations report that outcome rather than failing.",
            "A deadline is the one exception, and it is raised once. The wait whose clock ran out reports <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/errors/#TimeoutExpired\"><code>TimeoutExpired</code></a> after stopping and reaping the child; because that work is done, the outcome it produced is recorded, and a later wait, termination, or teardown returns it instead of re-raising an error about a child that is already gone."
          ]
        },
        "members": [
          {
            "name": "pid",
            "kind": "property",
            "display": "pid: int | None",
            "doc": {
              "summary": "The child's process identifier, as announced by its <code>start</code> event.",
              "sections": [
