      {
        "name": "StdinStream",
        "kind": "class",
        "module": "cuprum.sh.execution",
        "path": "cuprum/sh/execution.py",
        "line": 305,
        "display": "class StdinStream(\n    chunks: cabc.AsyncIterable[str | bytes] | cabc.AsyncIterator[str | bytes],\n)",
        "doc": {
          "summary": "A library-owned, pull-after-drain producer for stdin.",
          "body": [
            "Cuprum pulls one chunk from *chunks*, writes it to the child's stdin pipe, and waits for that write to drain before pulling the next. That is what lets a caller feed a child more data than they would ever hold in one buffer: the payload is never materialized whole. What it bounds is how far *ahead* the producer runs, not the size of a single chunk. While <code>drain()</code> waits, the chunk just pulled and its encoded payload are still in memory alongside the transport's write buffer and the OS pipe, so retained memory is roughly the largest chunk the producer yields plus those buffers. A caller who cares about peak memory should yield bounded-size chunks.",
            "The producer is advanced with <code>aiter()</code>, so *chunks* may be any async iterable. <code>str</code> chunks are encoded with the run's <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/commands/#ExecutionContext\"><code>ExecutionContext.encoding</code></a> and <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/commands/#ExecutionContext\"><code>ExecutionContext.errors</code></a>, incrementally, so a multi-byte character split across two chunks is still encoded correctly. <code>bytes</code> chunks are written verbatim.",
            "Cuprum owns the producer for the duration of the run. On every exit path -- normal completion, producer failure, exceeding the timeout, and cancellation -- it closes the child's pipe and then calls the iterator's <code>aclose()</code> when the producer provides one. That order is deliberate: the pipe is cuprum's own resource and the child's EOF depends on the close reaching it, so releasing it first means a caller's cleanup, however slow, cannot hold the child's input open.",
            "Both steps are bounded. Closing the pipe gives a graceful shutdown a short window and then releases the transport unconditionally, because a writer whose grandchild holds the read end open would otherwise wait forever. The producer's <code>aclose()</code> gets the same window: a finalizer that suspends longer than that is *cancelled*, not waited out, and one that absorbs cancellation keeps working in the background. So cuprum bounds its own wait rather than promising that a caller's cleanup has completed. A caller therefore does not need to finalize their own generator; a caller must not yield from a generator that something else is concurrently driving.",
            "A child that closes its stdin early (<code>head</code> is the canonical example) is normal rather than an error: the resulting <code>BrokenPipeError</code> is recorded as a <code>stdin_error</code> observation and the run continues to its exit code. Only producer and encoder failures raise <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/errors/#StdinSourceError\"><code>StdinSourceError</code></a>."
          ]
        },
        "members": [
          {
            "name": "chunks",
            "kind": "field",
            "display": "chunks: cabc.AsyncIterable[str | bytes] | cabc.AsyncIterator[str | bytes]",
            "doc": {
              "summary": "The async iterable or iterator whose chunks become the child's stdin."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
