            "name": "GroupTeardownOutcome",
            "kind": "class",
            "display": "GroupTeardownOutcome",
            "doc": {
              "summary": "Why a teardown could not establish settlement.",
              "body": [
                "A closed vocabulary because these values label a metric: an operator filters on the set of reasons a group was left behind, so every member is a stable, low-cardinality string that no runtime value can reach.",
                "<code>SIGNAL_REFUSED</code> is the one outcome emitted on both routes: it reports a signal the kernel refused rather than a count of surviving members.",
                "<code>UNSIGNALABLE</code> and <code>SETTLEMENT_TIMEOUT</code> both leave members running, and what separates them is what can still be done about it. An unsignalable group is outside this run's authority: nothing it sends reaches those members, so the teardown stops rather than waiting out a process it can never end. A settlement timeout was within reach in principle — the members were compelled with <code>SIGKILL</code> — and the capped wait expired because something outside this run owns their reaping. The first is a permissions fact about the group; the second is a race with an init process, and only the second is worth retrying."
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "name": "ResourceUsageMode",
            "kind": "class",
            "display": "ResourceUsageMode",
            "doc": {
              "summary": "How a terminal <code>exit</code> event obtained the resource figures it carries.",
              "body": [
                "A closed set, like <a href=\"/cuprum/docs/api/rust/#RustPumpDeclineReason\"><code>RustPumpDeclineReason</code></a>: the value is a metric label and a log extra that operators filter on, so a typo at a new call site would produce a value their filters silently miss. As an enum it is a type error instead.",
                "Members are <code>str</code>, so the observe event, the <code>cuprum_</code> log extras, the span attributes, and the metrics label all keep carrying plain strings — the constant this replaced was a <code>Literal</code> of exactly these three values, and a member formats as its own value."
              ],
              "examples": "The member value is the string consumers see::\n\n    assert ResourceUsageMode.WAIT4_CHILD == \"wait4_child\""
            }
          },
